# Parity test tolerance definitions: physically justified or hiding defects?
**Review ID**: test-03
**Category**: tests
**Date**: 2026-05-26
[0m
Review written to `docs/reviews/tests/test-03-parity-test-tolerance.md` (215 lines, 10 findings).

**Key findings:**

| Severity | Finding |
|---|---|
| Critical | All 11 fixtures are 1-hour simulations — tolerances named "annual" are misleadingly applied to 60-minute data |
| Critical | No `reference_output.parquet` files exist — parity tests never execute (dead code) |
| High | 25% HVAC/site-energy tolerances explicitly mask single-cycle phase offsets, not model errors |
| High | No automated tolerance-tightening mechanism exists (no CI test execution, no regression ratchet) |
| High | `relative_percent_deviation` uses `f64::EPSILON` (~2e-16) as near-zero guard — orders of magnitude too small |
| Medium | `ANNUAL_WATER_HEATER_ENERGY_REL_PCT_MAX` (0.5%) named as "annual" but applied to 1-hour data |
| Medium | 5% relative tolerance on small integer cycle counts produces binary pass/fail indistinguishable from equality |
| Medium | `BATTERY_SOC_MAE_ABS_MAX` has no physical justification comment |
| Low | Weather sky temperature diverges in `ochre_compat()` mode with no tolerance accounting |
| Low | `fixture_override` for cz2a_pv_ev (48%/43%) relies on manual removal, no automated regression check |
